WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.770
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:07.110
So I want to continue this
series on studying effectively.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:12.750
And whereas in the previous video I
talked about studying effectively in

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:18.510
general, and I'm referring of course to
studying mostly in a school environment

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:22.710
or a university environment, and in
the first video I talked about studying

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:26.910
effectively in general, in this video, I
want to talk about studying effectively

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:28.560
when it comes to learning languages.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:32.310
Remember if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe, click

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:34.200
on the bell to get notifications.

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:39.480
Why is it that so many people
struggle to learn languages at school?

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:44.370
Now I'm referring to the situation,
for example, in Canada, within the

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:49.769
English school system, where very
few, uh, outside of French immersion,

00:00:49.769 --> 00:00:53.355
which is where English speaking
kids do all of their schooling in

00:00:53.355 --> 00:00:54.885
French, which is a great program.

00:00:54.885 --> 00:00:59.985
And perhaps a few hundred, I don't know,
four or 500,000 kids every year do that.

00:01:00.315 --> 00:01:05.385
But in the regular French stream,
very few kids end up speaking French.

00:01:05.414 --> 00:01:06.765
I mean, that's just the reality.

00:01:07.125 --> 00:01:11.895
Uh, I don't know what the situation is in
the US with regard to foreign languages,

00:01:11.895 --> 00:01:13.935
Spanish or in the UK for that matter.

00:01:14.595 --> 00:01:18.780
I know that in countries like
Sweden, and in Europe, in many

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:20.820
countries in Europe, kids do learn.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:23.730
I'm not sure if they learn in school
or whether they learn because in

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:29.220
the case of Sweden, they have, um,
you know, uh, the, uh, television

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:33.120
programs, English or American
television programs are not dubbed.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:36.690
So they're provided in
the, uh, original version.

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:39.150
So the kids actually, before
they even start school,

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:40.140
they've heard a lot of English.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:41.040
And so they speak English.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:45.870
Well, But if I look at the situation
in Canada, uh, I think there are

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:50.430
a number of reasons why language
instruction in school is not effective.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:55.890
And it kind of goes against some of
the principles of effective study.

00:01:56.370 --> 00:02:02.790
So first of all, the emphasis in schools,
despite what people say is on tests.

00:02:03.464 --> 00:02:08.775
And it's on testing people
for specifics of grammar.

00:02:09.195 --> 00:02:16.424
So, um, I mentioned in that previous video
that in the UK for their, uh, geez, can't

00:02:16.424 --> 00:02:21.915
remember the GSE or whatever general,
you know, national tests that they have.

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:26.299
They were testing people on specific
points of grammar, and this would then

00:02:26.299 --> 00:02:31.730
determine whether the learner got their
diploma or didn't get their diploma.

00:02:32.149 --> 00:02:35.120
Issues of grammar that
are actually quite minor.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:36.530
They're there, yes.

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:42.410
For example, in French you say
"joue au football", "joue au..."

00:02:42.429 --> 00:02:46.989
whatever, you know, a sport,
for example, "joue du piano".

00:02:47.685 --> 00:02:51.375
But if you got that wrong, it
would not inhibit communication.

00:02:51.735 --> 00:02:56.145
If a person understands French well
and is able to communicate well and

00:02:56.145 --> 00:03:01.575
makes the odd mistake like that, it's
not that serious because the majority

00:03:01.575 --> 00:03:06.045
of students in the school system end
up not being able to speak at all,

00:03:06.225 --> 00:03:08.025
not being able to understand at all.

00:03:08.205 --> 00:03:12.885
So what's the possible point of testing
them on these specific points of grammar?

00:03:13.245 --> 00:03:19.305
All it does is introduce an element
of frustration, um, sense of failure.

00:03:19.695 --> 00:03:23.815
Uh, plus it's unfair because, okay,
as I pointed out in the earlier video,

00:03:24.145 --> 00:03:28.135
our brains are not so good at grabbing
these details, but are our brains will

00:03:28.135 --> 00:03:33.175
gradually develop a series of patterns
around how this foreign language works.

00:03:33.505 --> 00:03:37.465
And so given enough exposure
to the language, the learners

00:03:37.465 --> 00:03:39.265
will gradually get better.

00:03:39.685 --> 00:03:44.095
And if the goal is to be able to use
the language in a practical way to

00:03:44.095 --> 00:03:50.649
understand, to communicate meaning
then really testing people on specific

00:03:50.649 --> 00:03:54.899
points of grammar that are taught in
grade four, grade six or grade eight,

00:03:54.899 --> 00:03:58.739
and therefore right away, we will test
you on whether you learned it or not.

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:01.140
It's actually pointless.

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:04.140
It's just building in more
resistance to the language.

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:10.470
And again, referring to my earlier
video, we want learners to explore the

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:12.690
language, to cover the same ground.

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:16.050
Using different materials, different
stories, different content.

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:20.040
And of course the specific
grammatical points will show up.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:22.830
And these other, you know, uh, contexts.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:25.890
And the brain will gradually
start to pick up on them.

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:31.770
And it's possible that, uh, once the
brain has had enough experience with sort

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:35.870
of these aspects of the language, and if
the teacher points out that we say "joue

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:41.640
au", and in the case of a sport and "joue
du" in the case of a musical instrument

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:46.229
that that might help the learner remember
that, and then they might still get

00:04:46.229 --> 00:04:48.419
it wrong and it doesn't really matter.

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:55.200
So, uh, I think the, the, the problem
in, in language instruction in schools

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:58.919
is the teacher decides what the
learner is going to use as a textbook.

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:04.530
I've even heard teachers discourage
learners from going beyond

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:05.580
what the teacher is teaching.

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:09.120
The teacher wants to control
what the learner is learning.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:10.710
The content that learner is using.

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:16.080
Uh, again, on this, uh, exchange I saw
on Twitter, there's some discussion in

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:23.100
the UK as to which high frequency words
learners should have to demonstrate that

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:25.080
they know in order to get their diploma.

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:30.810
Well, the words that the learner knows
is going to reflect what they have read.

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:33.030
It's going to reflect what
they're interested in.

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:36.810
The fact that they don't know the
word "now", if they got into a

00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:40.710
situation where this word appeared
frequently, they'd learn it.

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:46.380
So again, I think that's an unnecessary,
specific detail to test people on

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:50.520
rather, how do you encourage people?

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:53.640
And this is difficult in the
case of French in Canada.

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:58.710
If you live in Vancouver, There's not an
awful lot of opportunity to use French,

00:05:58.950 --> 00:06:02.100
but the emphasis, the focus should be on

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:06.120
how do you get students to graze in the
language, to explore in the language?

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:10.290
Maybe even if you want them to learn
French, let them choose the language

00:06:10.290 --> 00:06:14.370
they want to learn, because if they
learn Spanish and they've got some

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:17.460
confidence that they can learn the
language, assuming they're more motivated

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:21.150
to learn Spanish or Chinese or whatever
it might be, if they achieve that,

00:06:21.435 --> 00:06:24.555
then it'll be easier for
them to learn French.

00:06:24.855 --> 00:06:30.855
In other words, effective study is based
on the idea that the student is motivated.

00:06:31.395 --> 00:06:32.985
And I know that's difficult.

00:06:32.985 --> 00:06:36.945
You've got a classroom of 25 kids that
aren't very motivated, but the challenge

00:06:36.974 --> 00:06:39.105
has to be how do we motivate these kids?

00:06:39.585 --> 00:06:45.344
If what we do in other words, insisting
on certain grammatical niceties of

00:06:45.344 --> 00:06:50.325
the language or a certain set of
words that have to be learned, and

00:06:50.325 --> 00:06:54.525
this will be the proof of whether
the student has achieved that level.

00:06:55.005 --> 00:06:59.865
If we are building in frustration
and disappointment and, uh, you

00:06:59.865 --> 00:07:04.425
know, resistance to the language,
um, we're not helping the situation.

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:07.500
And the proof is in the pudding
because at least in the case of

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:11.280
Canada, the vast majority of students
in the English language stream

00:07:11.790 --> 00:07:15.570
graduate after eight or 10 years of
French, and can't speak French, don't

00:07:15.570 --> 00:07:17.550
understand French, can't speak French.

00:07:17.940 --> 00:07:21.090
I mean, they speak some, they might
understand some, but essentially

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:23.070
they're not functional in French.

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:30.420
So I think that's why we have to
recognize that, that the brain doesn't

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:35.460
respond to this kind of, uh, you
know, you must learn this now and then

00:07:35.670 --> 00:07:40.080
you get the student to sit down and
try and block learn this thing now.

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:41.850
That's not how it works.

00:07:42.090 --> 00:07:45.240
Effective study involves
the sort of grazing.

00:07:45.540 --> 00:07:50.280
And also again, in, in school
instruction, the emphasis is, is

00:07:50.310 --> 00:07:52.830
A on grammar and B on speaking.

00:07:53.190 --> 00:07:56.700
They have this sort of role playing
and getting the students to speak.

00:07:56.760 --> 00:08:02.700
They have nothing to say until they
understand well and have a sufficient sort

00:08:02.700 --> 00:08:04.380
of level of experience with the language.

00:08:04.409 --> 00:08:05.520
They can't say that much.

00:08:06.030 --> 00:08:12.360
If they enjoy doing it, fine, but,
but that should not be in my view, the

00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:16.950
main, uh, sort of form of instruction,
emphasis on being able to say a few

00:08:16.950 --> 00:08:18.659
things, because once you have...

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:23.220
So you have an artificial situation in
the classroom where the student pretends

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:27.420
that they're a whatever, uh, you know,
a store clerk or buying something.

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:31.290
And once they're back in the real world,
none of that's gonna work for them.

00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:36.300
So it's far better to focus again,
as I said earlier, with sort of

00:08:36.300 --> 00:08:40.560
studying effectively, generally
improve the reading skill.

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:42.210
And how do you improve reading skills?

00:08:42.230 --> 00:08:43.170
By reading a lot.

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:46.080
So get them to read, let them
choose what they want to read.

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:47.370
It doesn't matter what they read.

00:08:47.550 --> 00:08:50.400
If they're reading a lot, they're going
to be improving their reading skills.

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:54.180
And get them to listen and listen
to material that they like.

00:08:54.210 --> 00:08:55.560
And to voices that they like.

00:08:55.860 --> 00:09:01.035
So, you know, they, the same idea that the
most efficient way to bring information

00:09:01.035 --> 00:09:05.855
into the brain, to stimulate the brain, to
get the brain to form these new patterns

00:09:06.215 --> 00:09:07.865
is through a lot of listening and reading.

00:09:08.105 --> 00:09:11.615
Of course, when opportunities are
there to speak, we should speak,

00:09:11.885 --> 00:09:16.085
but we shouldn't make that the
sort of main short term goal.

00:09:16.265 --> 00:09:18.425
It will eventually be the long-term goal.

00:09:18.574 --> 00:09:22.925
And yeah, once the brain has enough
experience with the language, has

00:09:22.925 --> 00:09:25.685
developed these patterns to cope
with that language, then of course

00:09:25.685 --> 00:09:30.645
we want to speak as much as possible,
but again, It should be meaningful.

00:09:30.645 --> 00:09:34.214
So meaningful conversation
talking about meaningful things.

00:09:34.485 --> 00:09:37.964
And if we will, you know, once we
have sufficient, a sufficient base

00:09:37.964 --> 00:09:41.745
in the language, we can help the
student find people if not in the

00:09:41.745 --> 00:09:46.485
classroom, even online that they can
talk to and talk in a meaningful way,

00:09:46.515 --> 00:09:48.755
not in a circle role-playing way.

00:09:49.025 --> 00:09:50.165
So there you go.

00:09:50.645 --> 00:09:55.985
My view on why language instruction
in the schools is not effective, at

00:09:55.985 --> 00:09:59.745
least in English-speaking Canada.

00:09:59.745 --> 00:10:03.375
And I have the impression from this
exchange I saw on Twitter there are

00:10:03.375 --> 00:10:05.745
serious problems in the UK as well.

00:10:05.745 --> 00:10:08.385
And if we look at the results in
terms of how many people speak a

00:10:08.385 --> 00:10:12.195
foreign language in those countries,
uh, hasn't been very effective.

00:10:12.675 --> 00:10:13.865
So thank you for listening.

00:10:13.865 --> 00:10:14.325
Bye for now.

